'''''Chiu Khéng-gio̍k''''' (周慶玉; 2 January 1505 – 11 April 1598), known among the [[Akwesa]] as Kengohka, was a merchant of [[Anping]] and the ruler of the polity of [[Okheng]] on the northwestern coast of [[Lurandia]]. He was the son of [[Chiu Bûn-hái]], the founder of the merchant house of the [[Sūn-heng Hâng]], and was married in 1521 to [[Chinowa (person)|Chinowa]], daughter of [[Hokta]], the miko of the [[Akwesa]].

He held four offices in succession. On the death of Hokta in 1527 he succeeded to the chieftainship of the Akwesa and became the ruler of Okheng; in 1535 he conveyed the territory to the [[Zong colonial administration]] and was appointed its provisional administrator; in 1543 he was removed from that office for his part in the [[Tamsui Plot]] and appointed ambassador to the Akwesa, a post he held for the remainder of his life.

== Early life ==

Chiu Khéng-gio̍k was born on 2 January 1505 at [[Huwei]], the capital of [[Zong]], the son of [[Chiu Bûn-hái]], a merchant of that city. He was six years old when his father took the grant of land at the bay of [[Oktalwa]] in 1511, and he appears in the record of the settlement from that period onwards, being brought up there rather than in Zong. He was sixteen at the time of his marriage. The Sūn-heng Hâng had operated a depot at the bay of [[Oktalwa]] since 1511, established under a grant of land from the Akwesa for ten pigs, and his father had by the 1520s built the outpost into the centre of a territory acquired through the company's support of the Akwesa in their wars against neighbouring [[Kawesa]] chiefdoms.

== Marriage ==

In 1521 he was married to Chinowa, the daughter of Hokta. The marriage was arranged between his father and Hokta.

He was known among the Akwesa as Kengohka, a name formed from the first element of his Zong name. The colonial papers do not record when it was given to him, and it is not stated whether it was adopted at the marriage or taken when he succeeded to the chieftainship. He is referred to by the Akwesa name in the records of the settlement and by his Zong name in the papers of the administration at Anping, the two usages corresponding to the two offices he held. It is described in the colonial papers as an agreement between the two older men and not as a matter between the couple; the later narrative of the marriage as a romance is not supported by the contemporary sources.

The Akwesa practised succession in the male line, and Hokta had no sons. The marriage therefore supplied the chiefdom with a male figure of standing connected to Hokta's line at the same time as it sealed the alliance between the chiefdom and the company.

== Miko of the Akwesa and ruler of Okheng ==

Hokta died in 1527 of plague, and Chiu Khéng-gio̍k succeeded him as miko. He was twenty-two. The office passed to the husband of Hokta's daughter rather than to a son of Hokta's line; the succession was determined during the outbreak that had killed Hokta and among a people that had lost many of its number to it, and the chiefdom was much reduced by those losses.

He held the title of miko for the remainder of his life, and retained it after the Akwesa ceased to be governed as a chiefdom, so that the office continued after the political entity to which it belonged had ended.

At the same time the territory passed under his rule as the polity of [[Okheng]], the joint holding of the merchant family and the chiefly family, which had been governed jointly since the marriage. The two offices were accordingly held by the same person: the rule of the merged territory, and the chieftainship of the people from whom it had been formed.

Plague recurred at the settlement in the years that followed, and the Akwesa continued to suffer heavy losses. No agreement existed with the colonial administration, the territory remitted no revenue to it, and the administration had judged the arrangement not worth managing when word of it reached Anping about 1522.

== Sale of the territory ==

In 1535 the colonial administration determined to acquire the site as the harbour for its northern presence. Chiu Khéng-gio̍k was paid 4,000 taels of gold and was at the same time threatened with invasion and with execution for treason should he refuse. The grounds for a charge existed in the unlicensed marriage, the conduct of diplomacy and war, and the dealing with a foreign people without authority. No charge was laid.

The sum was assessed as the value of the works the company had built — the wharves, warehouses, depot and other improvements — and no allowance was made for the land, for the political entity, or for the trade. The transaction was recorded as a purchase.

== Provisional administrator ==

He was not dispossessed. He and Chinowa remained in the territory, and he was appointed its provisional administrator. The appointment was made on the ground that the retention of the sitting administrator would present the transfer of authority more easily to the Akwesa, to the settlers and to the employees of the company than his removal. Zong became the parent polity of the territory, which remained a sub-territory of the Zong colony, and he continued in office as its provisional administrator.

== Tamsui Plot and removal ==

In 1543 he was removed from the administration. He had taken part in the [[Tamsui Plot]], in which the officials of the settlement declined to treat the orders of [[Anping]] as binding and the matter was carried to the [[Societal Assembly]].

He was arrested at Tamsui on 20 November 1543 by a force sent from Anping for the purpose, and held in custody until the royal edict of 1544 took effect in the colony on 11 November of that year, being about twelve months. He was not prosecuted for his part in the affair, and no penalty is recorded against him.

The office of provisional administrator was dissolved in 1544. It was replaced by the governorship of the [[Sinchew Colony]], to which [[Tân Bûn-khéng]] was appointed as its first holder. The division of the colonial territory into the Sinchew Colony and the [[Louyang Colony]], which followed within the year, owed its occasion to the same affair. The office that had accommodated the former ruler of an annexed territory was accordingly abolished with his departure, and the territory began under a governor with no connection to the people it contained.

Tân Bûn-khéng had been the administration's negotiator at the [[Treaty of Anping]] in 1538, where he had devised the posthumous title of King of the Northern Mahiks conferred on Hokta, and had fixed the boundary between the Zong territory and that of the [[Hachinawa|Hahkina]]. The man who devised the legal ground on which the coast was acquired took office over it five years later, on the removal of the man who had conveyed it.

== Ambassador to the Akwesa ==

After his removal he was appointed ambassador to the Akwesa, and he held the post for the remainder of his life. He was the only official of the colony with the qualification for it: he was married into the chiefly family, had lived at the settlement since childhood, and after 1527 had been the ruler of the people with whom he was required to deal.

The office is not otherwise described in the colonial papers, and the record of his tenure does not survive.

== Death ==

He died at [[Tamsui]] on 11 April 1598, aged 93, having held the office of ambassador to the Akwesa for fifty-five years. He had survived the chiefdom he had ruled by seventy-one years, the sale of its land by sixty-three, and his own father by fifty-five.

His life covered the whole of the colonial period in the north. He was born in the Zong capital four years before the first grant of land at the bay; he died in the settlement founded on it, in a colony that had been divided from Louyang and reconstituted along the [[Qingjiang]] river. He lived to see the [[Great Disappointment]] of 1564, the arrival of [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] explorers on the western coast; the [[Great Peasant Revolt]] of 1572 to 1585; the Baiduist usurpation of [[Foyuan of Zong|Foyuan]] and the [[Yuanlong Restoration]] that ended it; and the beginning of the [[Daaiho War]] in the [[The Jyunhap|Jyunhap]].

He did not return to Zong. He died at the settlement where he had been brought as a child and where he had spent the whole of his life after 1511.

== Family ==

He was married to [[Chinowa (person)|Chinowa]] from 1521 until her death in 1589, a period of sixty-eight years. She was born about 1507, the daughter of [[Hokta]], and died at [[Tamsui]] aged about 82. He survived her by nine years.

They had two children, neither of whom is named in the colonial papers. Through their mother they were the grandchildren of Hokta, the last miko of the Akwesa before him, and through their father the grandchildren of the merchant who founded the Sūn-heng Hâng. Any claim they had to the chieftainship was to an office that had lapsed, over a territory their father had conveyed to the colony. His father [[Chiu Bûn-hái]] appears less often in the record after the 1520s. He died at Tamsui on 9 June 1543, aged 69. His death fell two months before the discovery of the private trade at Tamsui that began the crisis of 1543–1544, and he did not live to see his son's removal or the division of the colony. Hokta's second daughter, [[Oktisa]], is recorded by name only.

== Historical record ==

Chiu Khéng-gio̍k appears in the colonial papers chiefly in the documents of the transactions of 1535 and 1543. The papers record the price paid, the offices conferred and withdrawn, and the terms of the treaty of 1538 that disposed of the coast on the strength of a title conferred on his father-in-law after death. They do not preserve his own account of any of these matters, and no document written by him or at his direction is known.

His career is the subject of a substantial later literature, most of it concerned with the sale of 1535 and the question of what choices were open to him. The evidence available consists of the administration's papers, which were written by the party to the transactions that dispossessed him.

